--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party font embedded in the distributed web verifier
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

web/inkan-verify.html and web/inkan-verify.artifact.html embed Inkan Sans as
base64 TrueType, so the page renders identically offline, from a static host,
and from an on-chain inscription.

    Inkan Sans, a Modified Version of IBM Plex Sans
    Copyright 2017 IBM Corp. with Reserved Font Name "Plex"
    (the font files' own notice: "Copyright 2018 IBM Corp. All rights reserved.")
    Licensed under the SIL Open Font License, Version 1.1
    http://scripts.sil.org/OFL

OFL condition 2 requires every redistributed copy to contain IBM's notice and
the licence. The full OFL text ships at LICENSE-FONTS, and each page also
reproduces it, with IBM's notice, inside its OFL-1.1 SPDX snippet beside the
font data, so a saved, re-hosted or inscribed copy of the page carries it. It
governs the embedded font data (that snippet in each page) and the subset font
files it is built from, wherever those appear. They are IBM's work, are NOT
part of the Licensed Work under LICENSE, are not BSL 1.1, and are not covered
by the page's Apache-2.0 grant.

The embedded copies are a Modified Version: subsets (Basic Latin, Latin-1
Supplement, and the handful of symbols the interface uses). IBM's licence
reserves the font name "Plex", and OFL section 3 forbids a Modified Version to
use a Reserved Font Name, so the subsets are renamed "Inkan Sans" in their
name tables and in the page's CSS. Apart from the subsetting itself, which
cut the glyph, character-map, metrics and layout tables down to the characters
kept, nothing else in the fonts was changed besides the vendor ID: the rename
leaves every one of those tables exactly as the subsetting produced it. IBM's
copyright notice is kept in each font, and the licence and its URL are restored
to the font's own name table. Inkan Sans is not endorsed by IBM.
The font is embedded as part of a larger work and is not sold on its own.
Characters outside the subset fall back to the host's system font.

